From c19afe491e5b88d4d16108412e8f267ed6f9131d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nik Samokhvalov <nik@postgres.ai>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 19:57:33 -0800
Subject: [PATCH v4 4/4] Look through typmod-only RelabelType on partition keys

The commit "Preserve typmod when reducing CASE, COALESCE, and NULLIF"
made eval_const_expressions() put a RelabelType on a rebuilt CASE,
COALESCE or NULLIF whose derived typmod differs from the original one,
for instance when an inlined SQL function is removed from its
arguments.  A partition key over such an expression now carries that
RelabelType in rel->partexprs, and so does the matching expression in
the query.  But three places strip RelabelType from the query
expression only, and then compare it with equal() to the key:

- gen_partprune_steps_internal() via match_clause_to_partition_key(),
  so plan-time and run-time partition pruning were lost;
- group_by_has_partkey(), so full partitionwise aggregation was lost;
- match_expr_to_partition_keys(), so partitionwise join was lost when
  the join clause operands are relabeled, as for varchar keys compared
  with the text equality operator.

To fix, look through a RelabelType on the partition key too, using a
new helper strip_typmod_relabel() that removes only RelabelType nodes
that keep the type and the collation.  Such a node changes neither the
value nor how it compares.  A RelabelType that changes the type or the
collation of the key is kept, so these matches do not get wider than
before: group_by_has_partkey() has no operator family check, and a
binary-compatible type with different equality semantics must not
match.

Re-simplifying the partition key with a valid root does not help here:
the key and the query expression already have the same RelabelType,
the mismatch comes from stripping it on one side only.

